# Next.js Patterns

## App Router (Next.js 16)
- Use Server Components by default
- Add `"use client"` directive only when necessary
- Use Suspense boundaries with fallback UI for async components
- Implement proper error boundaries with error.tsx
- Use loading.tsx for loading states

## Server Components (Default)
- Fetch data directly in the component
- Use async/await syntax
- No need for useState, useEffect for data fetching
- Can directly access database, environment variables, server-side APIs
- Keep Server Components as the default choice

Example:
```typescript
// app/capabilities/page.tsx
import { db } from "@/db"
import { capability } from "@/db/schema"

export default async function CapabilitiesPage() {
  const capabilities = await db.select().from(capability)

  return (
    <div>
      {capabilities.map(cap => (
        <CapabilityCard key={cap.id} capability={cap} />
      ))}
    </div>
  )
}
```

## Client Components
- Add `"use client"` at the top of the file
- Use for: interactivity, browser APIs, React hooks, event handlers
- Keep client components small and focused
- Prefer Server Components for data fetching, pass data as props to Client Components

Example:
```typescript
"use client"

import { useState } from "react"

export function InteractiveButton() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

  return (
    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
      Clicked {count} times
    </button>
  )
}
```

## Dynamic Imports
- Wrap client imports with dynamic imports when used in server components
- Prevents hydration mismatches
- Reduces initial bundle size

Example:
```typescript
import dynamic from "next/dynamic"

const ClientComponent = dynamic(() => import("@/components/client-component"), {
  loading: () => <Spinner />,
  ssr: false, // if component uses browser-only APIs
})
```

## Server Actions
- Place in `/src/actions/` directory
- Add `"use server"` directive at the top
- Export async functions
- Use Zod for input validation
- Return serializable data (no class instances, functions, etc.)
- Handle errors with try-catch, return error states
- Call `revalidatePath()` or `revalidateTag()` after mutations

Example:
```typescript
"use server"

import { revalidatePath } from "next/cache"
import { db } from "@/db"
import { capability } from "@/db/schema"

export async function createCapability(input: unknown) {
  try {
    // Validate input
    const validated = createCapabilitySchema.parse(input)

    // Insert into database
    const [newCapability] = await db
      .insert(capability)
      .values(validated)
      .returning()

    // Revalidate the capabilities page
    revalidatePath("/capabilities")

    return { success: true, data: newCapability }
  } catch (error) {
    return { success: false, error: "Failed to create capability" }
  }
}
```

## API Routes
- Use for: webhooks, third-party integrations, non-Next.js clients
- Prefer Server Actions for app mutations
- Return `Response` or use `NextResponse`
- Implement proper error handling (status codes)
- Validate inputs with Zod schemas

Example:
```typescript
// app/api/webhooks/route.ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from "next/server"

export async function POST(request: NextRequest) {
  try {
    const body = await request.json()

    // Process webhook

    return NextResponse.json({ success: true })
  } catch (error) {
    return NextResponse.json(
      { error: "Internal server error" },
      { status: 500 }
    )
  }
}
```

## Metadata
- Use `generateMetadata()` for dynamic metadata
- Export static metadata object for static pages
- Include OpenGraph and Twitter card metadata

Example:
```typescript
import type { Metadata } from "next"

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: "Capabilities | OpenModal.ai",
  description: "Track AGI capability development",
}

// Or dynamic
export async function generateMetadata({ params }): Promise<Metadata> {
  const capability = await getCapability(params.slug)

  return {
    title: capability.title,
    description: capability.description,
  }
}
```

## Route Groups
- Use route groups for layout organization: `(public)`, `(dashboard)`, `(auth)`
- Route groups don't affect URL structure
- Each group can have its own layout

## Loading and Error States
- Create `loading.tsx` for route loading states
- Create `error.tsx` for error boundaries
- Use Suspense for component-level loading

## Performance
- Use Next.js Image component for all images
- Implement lazy loading with Suspense
- Minimize client-side JavaScript
- Use React Query's caching for client-side data

## Revalidation
- Use `revalidatePath("/path")` after mutations to specific paths
- Use `revalidateTag("tag")` for more granular control
- Set revalidation time in route segment config for time-based ISR

Example:
```typescript
// app/capabilities/[slug]/page.tsx
export const revalidate = 3600 // Revalidate every hour
```
